Q1. Deadlock Debugging 
Deadlock Condition:
P1 locks R1 and waits for R2.
P2 locks R2 and waits for R1.
Each process holds one resource while waiting for the other, creating a circular wait, which leads to a deadlock.
Optimization:
Enforce a fixed resource ordering.
Both processes should acquire resources in the same order (e.g., always lock R1 first, then R2).
This removes the circular wait condition without changing the process logic.

Q2. CPU Utilization Problem 
Cause:
Processes repeatedly move between Ready and Waiting, indicating frequent I/O operations.
The CPU stays idle while processes wait for I/O, resulting in only 30% CPU utilization.
System-Level Optimization:
Use asynchronous I/O or DMA to overlap I/O with CPU execution.
Increase the degree of multiprogramming so another ready process can execute while one waits for I/O.
This improves CPU utilization
.
Q3. Memory Bottleneck Debugging 
Memory Issue:
Frequent page faults occur because the large heap (150 MB) and 25 thread stacks (25 MB) cause poor memory locality and excessive paging.
Constant page loading slows program execution (thrashing).
Optimizations:
Reduce memory usage by optimizing heap allocation and reusing memory.
Reduce the number of threads or decrease stack size/use a thread pool to lower memory consumption and page faults. 

Q4. Starvation Debugging 
Root Cause:
P5 is in Q3 (lowest priority).
Continuous arrival of higher-priority processes in Q1 and Q2 prevents Q3 from getting CPU time.
This causes starvation.
Optimization:
Apply aging by gradually increasing the priority of waiting processes.
Alternatively, reserve CPU time periodically for lower-priority queues to ensure fairness.

Q5. Context-Switch Overhead Debugging 
Reason for High Context Switching:
The 1 ms time quantum is shorter than the 2 ms average CPU burst, so threads are preempted before completing.
Frequent I/O also causes many process state changes, increasing context switches.
Optimizations:
Increase the time quantum (e.g., 4–5 ms) to reduce unnecessary preemptions.
Improve I/O handling (e.g., asynchronous I/O or batching I/O requests) to reduce blocking and context switches.
